Given an integer n, find whether it is a power of 4 or not.Example :Input : n = 16Output : trueExplanation: 16 is a power of 4Input : 20Output : falseExplanation: 20 is not a power of 4Try It YourselfTable of ContentUsing Bit Manipulation - O(log n) Time and O(1) SpaceLogarithmic Approach - O(1) Time and O(1) SpaceBit Masking Technique - O(1) Time and O(1) SpacePerfect Square + Modulo Approach - O(1) Time and O(1) SpaceUsing Bit Manipulation - O(log n) Time and O(1) SpaceThe idea is to first check whether the number is a power of 2 (i.e., it has only one set bit), and then ensure that the position of this set bit is at an even index (counting from 0), which guarantees it is a power of 4. This can be verified using bit operations by confirming that n & (n - 1) == 0 and the set bit lies at an even position.
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

bool isPowerOfFour(int n) {
    int count = 0;

    // Check if n is power of 2 (only one set bit)
    if (n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0) {

        // Count number of shifts (position of set bit)
        while (n > 1) {
            n >>= 1;
            count++;
        }

        // Check if position is even
        return (count % 2 == 0);
    }

    return false;
}

int main() {
    int n = 64;

    if (isPowerOfFour(n))
        cout << "true";
    else
        cout << "false";

    return 0;
}


